  "surprise": "Batch 128 -> 192 (only edit; 4,800,000 params identical to 136) scored 643/616/623 on seeds 0/1/2 vs 136-fable's 655/673/646: exact means 627.33 vs 658.00, -30.67, every seed lower (paired -12/-57/-23), inside the registered -35..+30 range (central +3) but near its bottom. Discard. The registered mechanism is falsified on its own measurement: pretraining ran 2255/2218/2412 steps vs 136's 3370/3351/3367 (-33/-34/-28%) against a registered 2,700-3,050 (the pre-registered falsifier 'steps < 2,600' fired), i.e. step time scaled ~1.4-1.5x with 1.5x rows, so the step is token-bound, not overhead-bound. 146-fable, published while this was being registered, found the same from the other side (removing the Muon dispatch and host sync bought only +5% steps). My reading of 103/124 was wrong in the specific: MLP width barely moves the step, but rows x padded length do, so the per-token cost is mostly non-MLP work (attention with the float ALiBi bias, Peri-LN's four LayerNorms per block plus the MTP block, activation memory traffic); unprofiled, interpretation only. Records seen were therefore unchanged (432,960/425,856/463,104 vs 431,360; 2.81/2.77/3.01 epochs vs 2.80), which makes this a clean 'same data, one third fewer and 1.5x larger updates' ablation: pretraining matched 136 (final val 0.080/0.085/0.085 vs 0.086/0.083/0.079; held-out greedy 0.9715/0.966/0.9655 vs 0.965/0.9705/0.968) and RL-stage targets solved after round 4 matched (1125/1091/1106, mean 1107 vs 1113; seed 0 led 136's seed 0 in every round, 628/919/1062/1125 vs 577/878/1013/1102), yet dev >= 7 fell on every seed. At one-seed sd ~44 the -31 is about 1.2 standard errors of a 3-seed mean, so the honest read is no gain from a larger batch at equal data, consistent with 066/067/092 leaving 128 as the optimum. Next speed lead given a token-bound step: cut padded token slots without a per-row bias, e.g. sort each step's 128 records by length and run two micro-batches that each keep the broadcast (1,